{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/User Story.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/User Story.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"user story user story","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/User Story.md","fileName":"User Story.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"User Story","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 15:58\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Agile Backlog and Estimation]] [[Software Requirements Elicitation and Specification]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Agile Backlog and Estimation","Software Requirements Elicitation and Specification","Acceptance Criteria","agilegamedevelopment2e.pdf","hands-onsoftwarearchitecturewithc8andnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/User Story.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/User Story.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"user story user story","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/User Story.md","fileName":"User Story.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"User Story","heading":"User Story","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A user story describes desired value from a user's point of view, commonly as “As a role, I want a goal, so that a reason.” It is a concise placeholder for a conversation, not a complete specification.\n\nThe form keeps attention on who benefits and why. [[Acceptance Criteria]] add testable detail when the story approaches implementation, while the Development Team retains freedom to discover the solution.\n\nWithin architecture work, a story is one input to requirements analysis: its concise value statement can reveal behavior, but performance, security, interoperability, and other cross-cutting constraints may need separate specification.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Agile Backlog and Estimation","Software Requirements Elicitation and Specification","Acceptance Criteria","agilegamedevelopment2